name: dotnet-testing-filesystem-testing-abstractions

Test file/directory/path operations with `System.IO.Abstractions`; refactor static `System.IO` usage, write filesystem unit tests, simulate states and exceptions, and design testable file-processing services. Keywords: `IFileSystem`, `MockFileSystem`, `File.ReadAllText`, `File.WriteAllText`, `Directory.CreateDirectory`, `Path.Combine`.

Real disk I/O is slower and environment-dependent, causes side effects/races, and makes errors hard to simulate. `IFileSystem` exposes `File`, `Directory`, `FileInfo`, `DirectoryInfo`, `Path`, and `DriveInfo`.

Packages:
```xml
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions" Version="21.*" />
<PackageReference Include="System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers" Version="21.*" />
```

Inject `IFileSystem` instead of using static classes:
```csharp
public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string LoadConfig(string path) => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);
}
```
DI:
```csharp
services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
services.AddScoped<ConfigService>();
```

Use `MockFileSystem` to seed files/directories and verify reads, writes, copies, metadata, backups, and line counts:
```csharp
var fs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    ["config.json"] = new MockFileData("{ \"key\": \"value\" }"),
    [@"C:\logs\"] = new MockDirectoryData()
});
var service = new ConfigService(fs);
```
After writes, verify with `mockFileSystem.File.Exists(...)` and `ReadAllTextAsync(...)`. For copies, assert target directory and file exist. Test metadata (`Name`, `Size`) and backup path/creation as needed.

For unsupported exception simulation, use NSubstitute:
```csharp
var fs = Substitute.For<IFileSystem>();
var file = Substitute.For<IFile>();
fs.File.Returns(file);
file.Exists("protected.txt").Returns(true);
file.ReadAllText("protected.txt").Throws(new UnauthorizedAccessException("Access denied"));
```
Assert service behavior (e.g., false and null content). Cover `IOException`, `DirectoryNotFoundException`, and `UnauthorizedAccessException`.

Use `_fileSystem.Path.Combine(...)`; check `_fileSystem.File.Exists(path)`; create missing parent directories using `Path.GetDirectoryName`, `Directory.Exists`, and `Directory.CreateDirectory`. Handle relevant I/O exceptions. Use an independent `MockFileSystem` per test.

**Hardcode path separators** — Don't do this: `"configs\\app.json"` or `"configs/app.json"`; use `Path.Combine`.
**Use real file system in unit tests** — use `MockFileSystem`, not `new FileSystem()`.
**Ignore exception handling** — check existence and handle exceptions; do not assume files exist.

**Only create files necessary for testing**
**Avoid simulating oversized files in tests**
For large-file logic, use moderate data, e.g. 1,000 lines with `Enumerable.Range` and `mockFileSystem.AddFile`.

Benefits: 10–100x faster, reliable, isolated tests and precise error simulation.

References: `templates/configmanager-service.cs` (load/save, JSON, directories, backups); `templates/filemanager-service.cs` (copy, backup, metadata, errors). Related skills: `nsubstitute-mocking`, `unit-test-fundamentals`. Source: kevintsengtw/dotnet-testing-agent-skills (MIT), ported to dotnet-agent-harness. Day 17 article: https://ithelp.ithome.com.tw/articles/10375981; samples: https://github.com/kevintsengtw/30Days_in_Testing_Samples/tree/main/day17.
